WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.000
The principle that we studied in about two mishna-yom is that there is a limitation how far can we go on yom tov.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:34.000
And this depends on whether I make a yom or not a yom. And different people may make a yom in different directions.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Now, if I have water from a well, so if the well is of care, then I can take the water as far as I can go.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:01.000
If I take water from his private, from him, so then I can take it only as far as he can go.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Suppose he cannot go to the east because he didn't make a yom to the east, then he wouldn't be able to take it.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Now, question. What about a well which is public, hefkeb, for ole vabel, ole vabel means for any pilgrims, for the pilgrim, it's hefkeb.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Now for whatever reason I cannot go down the well, I don't have the instruments, and he is kind enough to go down and take the water.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.000
If he takes it for himself, then I will be stuck because I can only go as far as he can go.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
And I want to go to the east and he cannot go to the east. So now what does he do?

00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:00.000
He says, when he takes this water, he says, and he is of care and said, for me, is this good or not?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Are you allowed to make this sort of kinyan?

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Yes, in theory you could. So I am of care, this is for Rabbi Faul.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Can we just say shleponon?

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
No, I mean, if I would be taking water for myself, I could take water for myself, so hefkeb.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
So here also, there is no instruments of Rabbi Faul. Kinyan assured is commercial kinyan.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.000
This I am taking water, I am drawing water for myself, so therefore I can go to the east.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Because I could go to the east, I could take this water with me to the east.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Now he cannot go to the east. So now he is going down the well, and drawing water from the well,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
or going down the well, it depends on what kind of well.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:03.000
And he says, this water is of care for Rabbi Faul, and he is of care for this.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Now, one second. The question is, is this considered mine already?

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:16.000
I swear he took it, and therefore I could take it to the east, or no?

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Is it considered his? Because he didn't give it to me.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Now when he gives it to me, what's his?

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.000
And already the water could not go to the east, because he cannot go to the east.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.000
I understand the problem.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Now, there is a mahloket.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:56.000
And the Gemara says, this mahloket is mahloket to whom it belongs.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.000
And now, the Gemara says like this, this mahloket is studying something else.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:17.000
Suppose I find here a boba, a dog.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.000
I don't have children here, I don't have grandchildren.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.000
So I see it, and I say, oh, this is for Yaakov Ovadia.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.000
This is for your child.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.000
What's that, Messiah?

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.000
I'm not going to use it.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.000
But I know you like it, and your son will use it.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.000
I say, at the moment I need this mahloket for Yaakov.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.000
I might be a Messiah to his friend.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Because his friend is the mahloket of the Gemara.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Right.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.000
That's what the Gemara says.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.000
So, some people say, I can't take, the person that said that when I can take the water, it's for you,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.000
believes that I might be a Messiah to his friend.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.000
And the person that says no, if I took the water, and I said it's for you, it's not for you.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.000
This person believes, I might be a Messiah to his friend, not for his friend.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.000
Right.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.000
Very good.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:31.000
Great, and now, Rabi Rabit is going to read for us.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.000
So here is what the Gemara says.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.000
Now, the Messiah,

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.000
to his friend,

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.000
or his friend,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.000
to his friend,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.000
we don't, we don't call it that.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Why?

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.000
We don't call it that.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.000
We don't call it that also,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:52.000
in the Gemara says, in Mosavar,

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:54.000
yes.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.000
We don't call it that.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Why?

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.000
We don't call it that also, in the Gemara says, in Mosavar,

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.000
yes.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.000
In Mosavar, we don't call it that.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.000
In Mosavar, we don't call it that.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.000
We don't call it that.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Why?

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.000
Yes.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.000
Yes.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Yes.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.000
Here it says,

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.000
what is it?

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Here it says,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.000
to his friend,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.000
and

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:29.000
I'm sorry,

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.000
I'm talking badly.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.000
One second.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.000
Yes, Rahman.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Because I don't want to be in contradiction with Rahman,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.000
because Rahman is saying

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.000
the opposite.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.000
One second.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Yes.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.000
Because here it says, Rahman,

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.000
that the water belongs to me.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.000
I mean, it could be back there to me.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.000
And in Bahman we say,

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.000
Rahman says,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.000
look at the water.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.000
Look at the water.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.000
So therefore, since there is a contradiction,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.000
look at the water.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.000
And then it goes to the previous experience.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.000
One second.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.000
Now,

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.000
Yes.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.000
Not only was it changed to the original text,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.000
I want to show you something very interesting.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:51.000
Yes.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.000
No.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.000
The original text is from the Toshfot.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.000
From the Toshfot,

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.000
and from Aravat.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.000
I want you to be patient with me,

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.000
because I was...

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Ah.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.000
And

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.000
there is a second Aravat.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.000
Yes.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.000
And the Aravat, very properly,

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.000
caused a

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.000
deriv.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.000
We don't have a deriv for that no more.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.000
The deriv also was erased.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.000
All right.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.000
Why not?

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.000
And it says,

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.000
the last line,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.000
the one line before the last.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.000
Something like that.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.000
Then the Aravat says,

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.000
I had kushan for the deriv.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.000
Because the deriv makes it

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.000
